PLACES=[
N('southfield-furnace-ruin','Southfield Furnace ruin','Southfields, Town of Tuxedo','midh','industry','ruins',41.25389,-74.18250,'site','public','walk','','1804–1887',
  'Peter Townsend II, who also owned the mines, built an iron furnace here on Mombasha Creek in the first years of the 19th century (Wikipedia files it under 1804; the Trail Conference says it was built in 1805 and rebuilt in 1836). Besides the furnace, the Southfield Ironworks had a stamping mill, grist mill, saw mill, smith and wheelwright shops, a coal shed, a store and stables. The furnace was last fired in September 1887. It was listed on the National Register in 1973.',
  'The furnace stack, the stone-arch charging bridge and several walls of the casting room still stand beside the red-blazed Furnace Loop Trail, which then follows an old railroad bed (Trail Conference hike description). The ruin is on the north side of Orange Turnpike (County Route 19), 0.7 mile northwest of NY 17.',
  'An 1800s iron furnace with its stone charging bridge, five minutes from a parking area in Sterling Forest.',
  SFACC+'From NY 17 in Southfields take Orange Turnpike (County Route 19) 0.6 mile and turn left onto Hall Drive: the parking area is on the left at a curve. Walk north up Hall Drive on the white Wildcat Mountain Trail, cross Orange Turnpike, step over the guardrail, cross the footbridge over Mombasha Creek and turn right on the red Furnace Loop Trail (Trail Conference). Dogs on leash. Ruined masonry: keep off the walls.',
  [W('Southfield Furnace Ruin'),L('NY-NJ Trail Conference: Furnace Loop/Indian Hill Loop Trails from Hall Road','https://secure.nynjtc.org/hike/print/6126'),SFTC,SF],
  th=[41.25291,-74.18310],thn='Hall Drive parking area (Sterling Forest)',brick='no'),
N('sterling-furnace-lakeville','Sterling Furnace ruins (Lakeville Ironworks)','Sterling Forest, Town of Warwick','midh','industry','ruins',41.19761,-74.26016,'site','public','walk','','1751–1804',
  'Cornelius Board and Timothy Ward started an ironworks at the outlet of Sterling Lake in 1736, using the lake\'s ore, timber and water power (NYS Parks). The dates of the furnace differ by source: the 1906 Daughters of the Revolution tablet says it was built on this spot in 1751; Atlas Obscura says William and Abel Noble built the first full-size furnace in 1751 and Peter Townsend a second, larger one in 1770; the Trail Conference gives 1770, abandoned in 1804. Under Townsend in 1778 the works made the great chain stretched across the Hudson at West Point, 770 links of about 140 pounds each, forged in some six weeks.',
  'The ruins of the furnace lie just below the junction of the blue Sterling Lake Loop and the yellow Lakeville Ironworks Trail, a short walk from the visitor center past the stone foundations of a former church (Trail Conference). The Lakeville Ironworks Trail (0.6 mile) has interpretive signs, and the 1906 bronze tablet stands by the path. Atlas Obscura users\' photographs show a restored furnace stack.',
  'Where the iron for Washington\'s Hudson River chain was smelted, a short walk from a state park visitor center.',
  SFACC+'Park in the lot on Old Forge Road across from the visitor center, go out the visitor center\'s front entrance and follow the blue Sterling Lake Loop across the field to the yellow Lakeville Ironworks Trail; the easy loop is about 1.1 miles (Trail Conference). Ruined masonry: keep off the walls.',
  [SF,SFHIKE,SFTC,HM('Sterling Furnace',180865),L('Atlas Obscura: Sterling Lake Iron Works','https://assets.atlasobscura.com/places/sterling-lake-iron-works')],
  th=[41.19868,-74.25692],thn='Sterling Forest visitor center lot, Old Forge Road',conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('sterling-lake-mine-ruins','Sterling and Lake Mine plant ruins','Sterling Forest, Town of Warwick','midh','industry','ruins',41.19869,-74.26293,'site','public','walk','','1736–1917',
  'Iron ore was found round Sterling Lake as early as 1736, and mining went on here until 1917 (Trail Conference). The Sterling and Lake mines both ran under the lake; the plant whose ruins stand by the road was built to serve them. The land came to belong to the Sterling Iron Works, and the last mines in what is now the state park closed in the 1920s (Wikipedia).',
  'Concrete-and-brick ruins on the left of the paved road that the blue Sterling Lake Loop follows after it crosses the lake outlet on a wooden bridge, just before the road runs along the lake shore (Trail Conference). OpenStreetMap maps four ruined buildings and a chimney here. Atlas Obscura users\' photographs show a flooded entrance to the Lake Mine.',
  'The concrete shell of the plant that worked two iron mines running under Sterling Lake.',
  SFACC+'From the visitor center lot on Old Forge Road follow the blue Sterling Lake Loop past the Sterling Furnace and over the outlet bridge, then right along the road (Trail Conference). Old mine openings are flooded and dangerous: stay on the road and out of the ruins.',
  [SFHIKE,W('Sterling Forest State Park'),SF,L('Atlas Obscura: Sterling Lake Iron Works','https://assets.atlasobscura.com/places/sterling-lake-iron-works')],
  th=[41.19868,-74.25692],thn='Sterling Forest visitor center lot, Old Forge Road',brick='?'),
N('sterling-forest-fire-tower','Sterling Mountain fire tower and observer\'s cabin','Sterling Forest, Town of Warwick','midh','landmark','standing',41.19526,-74.27844,'exact','public','hike','','1922',
  'A 60-foot steel Aermotor lookout tower put up in 1922 on Sterling Mountain, at 1,320 feet, with an observer\'s cabin of board and batten built about 1934. An access road was made in 1968, when both were repaired. New York ended its fire-tower program in 1989, and the station became a volunteer-run reserve lookout. It was listed on the National Register in 2006.',
  'The tower, the ranger cabin and a picnic table at its foot on the Sterling Ridge Trail. From the top: Sterling Lake to the northeast, Cedar Pond to the south, part of Greenwood Lake to the west, and on clear days the Beacon mountains, Schunemunk and the Catskills (Trail Conference). The Trail Conference notes the tower is closed to the public when not manned.',
  'A 1922 steel fire tower over Sterling Lake, still kept as a lookout by volunteers.',
  SFACC+'About 4.3 miles round trip from the visitor center lot on Old Forge Road: blue Sterling Lake Loop, orange Bare Rock Trail up the ridge, then south on the blue-on-white Sterling Ridge Trail; or 2.1 miles from the Fire Tower Trail\'s eastern trailhead on Long Meadow Road, which has limited parking. The Trail Conference says to check with the park office that the tower is open before you go.',
  [W('Sterling Mountain Fire Observation Tower and Observer\'s Cabin'),SFHIKE,SFTC,SF],
  th=[41.19868,-74.25692],thn='Sterling Forest visitor center lot, Old Forge Road',brick='no'),
N('clove-furnace-arden','Clove Furnace','Arden, Town of Tuxedo','midh','industry','ruins',41.27361,-74.14945,'site','open','drive','','1854–1877 or 1885',
  'Robert and Peter Parrott built this anthracite blast furnace in 1854 as part of their Greenwood Iron Works, whose iron went to the West Point Foundry at Cold Spring, maker of the Parrott rifle used by the Union Army. It was of stone, 54 feet high and 37 feet square at the base (Orange County Historical Society). The sources disagree on the end: Wikipedia says it shut down for good soon after Robert Parrott died in 1877, the Hudson River Valley Heritage Area says 1885. The older charcoal Greenwood Furnace of 1811, about half a mile away, also stands in crumbling remains.',
  'The furnace ruin stands on Arden Station Road just east of the Thruway and can be seen from the highway (Wikipedia). The site is the headquarters of the Orange County Historical Society. Interior of the society\'s buildings not checked.',
  'The stone stack that smelted iron for the Parrott guns, now the county historical society\'s home.',
  'Orange County Historical Society, 21 Clove Furnace Drive, Arden. The society says it is open only by appointment, on Wednesday mornings (its home page, read November 21, 2026). Parking at the site not checked.',
  [W('Clove Furnace Ruin'),L('Orange County Historical Society: The Clove Furnace','https://orangecountyhistoricalsociety.org/CloveFurnace.html'),L('Orange County Historical Society: Clove Furnace, continued','https://orangecountyhistoricalsociety.org/CloveFurnace_p2.html'),L('Orange County Historical Society (home page, hours)','https://orangecountyhistoricalsociety.org/'),L('Hudson River Valley Heritage Area: Clove Furnace','https://www.hudsonrivervalley.com/sites/Clove-Furnace-/details')],
  conf='mixed',brick='no',hours='By appointment, Wednesday mornings'),
N('pine-swamp-mine-harriman','Pine Swamp Mine and miners\' village','Harriman State Park, Town of Tuxedo','midh','industry','ruins',41.24917,-74.11381,'site','public','hike','','about 1830–1880',
  'An iron mine opened about 1830 and worked off and on until 1880, beside the Arden-Surebridge Trail in Harriman State Park. Its workers lived in a small village just below the mine openings (Trail Conference).',
  'A large rectangular cut in the hillside left of the trail just past a stream crossing below a cascade, then several more excavations and open pits now full of water; beyond them a stone wall and several stone foundations of the miners\' village. The same loop passes a shaft of the Hogencamp Mine (1870–1885) near the boulder called Cape Horn, and stone foundations probably left from that mine\'s tramway.',
  'Water-filled iron pits and the stone foundations of the village where the miners lived.',
  'Harriman State Park (Palisades Interstate Park Commission), open every day year-round from dawn to dusk (NYS Parks page, read November 21, 2026). Park at the Lake Skannatati lot on Seven Lakes Drive, 0.7 mile beyond the Kanawauke Circle; the Trail Conference\'s 3.7-mile Iron Mines Short Loop goes out on the aqua Long Path and back on the Arden-Surebridge Trail past the mine. Dogs on leash. Open mine pits and shafts: stay out of them.',
  [L('NY-NJ Trail Conference: Iron Mines Short Loop','https://secure.nynjtc.org/hike/print/1892'),HSP],
  th=[41.24200,-74.10228],thn='Lake Skannatati parking area, Seven Lakes Drive',brick='no'),
N('tuxedo-station','Tuxedo station (Tuxedo Park Railroad Station)','Tuxedo','midh','rail','standing',41.19416,-74.18440,'exact','view','drive','stations','1885',
  'The Erie Railroad opened a stop here, called Lorillard\'s, in the spring of 1880 on land of the Lorillard family. When the Lorillards began Tuxedo Park in 1885, the depot was replaced with a new building designed by Bruce Price, one of the original Tuxedo Park buildings, counted among the better stations on the Erie. The station agent went in August 1966. The building was listed on the National Register in 2000 and renovated in the early 2010s.',
  'The 1885 depot beside the single track of Metro-North\'s Port Jervis Line, the only stop on the line that keeps its old station at the working station site (Wikipedia). Whether the building is open inside was not checked.',
  'Bruce Price\'s 1885 depot for the gated resort of Tuxedo Park, still beside a working platform.',
  'An active commuter station of Metro-North\'s Port Jervis Line (run by NJ Transit), on the east side of NY 17 south of East Village Road. It has a 245-space lot and a separate state lot; the station is also the western end of the Ramapo-Dunderberg Trail into Harriman State Park (Wikipedia). Stay off the track. Station parking rules not checked.',
  [W('Tuxedo station')],
  brick='?'),
N('smith-clove-meetinghouse','Smith Clove Meetinghouse','Highland Mills','midh','worship','standing',41.36111,-74.11139,'site','view','drive','','1802',
  'Quakers in Smith\'s Clove met in a member\'s home until they bought this land in 1801; the meeting house went up at the beginning of the 19th century (the Photo News gives 1802). It is the oldest religious building in the town and village of Woodbury. Renovated in the mid-1800s and otherwise unaltered, it is still used by the Cornwall Friends Meeting about once a year. It was listed on the National Register in 1974.',
  'A one-and-a-half-story clapboard meeting house on a fieldstone foundation, with a central chimney, a porch along the south front and two doors, one at each end, from the time when men and women met separately. Inside: one sparely finished room split by a removable wooden partition, plastered walls, wide plank floors and plain wooden benches, each half heated by a wood stove. A small burial ground of early members lies just north.',
  'An 1802 Quaker meeting house with its men\'s and women\'s doors and sliding partition intact.',
  'Cornwall Monthly Meeting of the Society of Friends, which holds its weekly worship in Cornwall and comes here for one summer Sunday a year (in 2012 the house was open 10:30 a.m. to 1 p.m. on August 26, with worship 11 to noon; this year\'s date not checked). Otherwise view from Quaker Avenue, just off NY 32 on Smith Clove Road; the grounds were not checked for public access.',
  [W('Smith Clove Meetinghouse'),L('Photo News: Area Quakers will hold services at historic Smith Clove Meeting House (2012)','https://www.thephoto-news.com/entertainment/area-quakers-will-hold-services-at-historic-smith-clove-meeting-house-IRPN20120815120819977'),L('Cornwall Friends Meeting','https://cornwallquakers.org')],
  brick='no',hours='?'),
N('rushmore-memorial-library','Rushmore Memorial Library','Highland Mills','midh','civic','standing',41.34924,-74.12523,'exact','view','drive','','1923–1924',
  'A small Arts and Crafts library of the 1920s, paid for by Charles E. Rushmore, the local resident Mount Rushmore is named for; the marker says the Rushmore family built it in 1923. It was the Town of Woodbury\'s public library until a larger one was built in the mid-1980s, and is now the town historical society\'s headquarters. It was listed on the National Register in 2008.',
  'A one-story building of local uncoursed puddingstone with a Mission-style tiled hipped roof that overhangs on all sides and over the front steps, and a stone chimney. Inside: one room with a big stone chimney breast and fireplace, mahogany bookshelves, original trim, and a bronze plaque with a poem by Rushmore\'s daughter, Jean Rushmore Patterson (Wikipedia). When the historical society opens it was not checked.',
  'The puddingstone library given by the man Mount Rushmore is named after.',
  'Woodbury Historical Society headquarters, at NY 32 and Weygant Hill Road (543 NY 32). View from the road; the society\'s opening hours were not checked.',
  [W('Rushmore Memorial Library'),HM('Rushmore Library',45986)],
  brick='no',hours='?'),
N('museum-village-monroe','Museum Village of Old Smith\'s Clove','Monroe','midh','town','standing',41.34501,-74.19957,'site','open','walk','','1950',
  'An open-air museum of 19th-century rural life founded by Roscoe William Smith, who started the Orange and Rockland electric company in 1905 and gave the village to the local community, by the museum\'s own account his most cherished gift (Hudson River Valley Institute). It has served the valley since 1950.',
  'A village of 19th-century buildings with a general store, drug store, cloth-making, candle-dipping, a blacksmith and an old printing shop, with demonstrations (Hudson River Valley Institute). Which buildings are old and which were moved in was not checked.',
  'A whole 19th-century village assembled by the founder of the local power company.',
  'Museum Village, 1010 NY 17M, Monroe. Adults $10, seniors and students $5, children 10 and under free; no pets except service dogs (its hours page, read November 21, 2026). Its home page says the website is being rebuilt; the Hudson River Valley Institute\'s page gives different hours and fees, so go by the museum\'s own.',
  [L('Museum Village: Hours and admission','https://museumvillage.org/?p=349'),L('Hudson River Valley Institute: Museum Village','https://www.hudsonrivervalley.org/museum-village')],
  brick='?',hours='July 9 to August 30, Thursday to Sunday 11 a.m. to 4 p.m.; spring and fall, Saturday and Sunday 11 a.m. to 4 p.m.; winter events'),
N('hathorn-stone-house-warwick','General John Hathorn Stone House','Town of Warwick','midh','landmark','standing',41.24532,-74.37066,'exact','view','drive','','1773',
  'John Hathorn, who had surveyed the New York-New Jersey line as a young man, settled in Warwick, opened a store and an iron forge, and built this stone house in 1773. He commanded the Warwick militia in the Revolution, led it at Minisink, and later sat in the state legislature and Congress. The house was much enlarged in the mid-19th century and was listed on the National Register in 2001 with an early-19th-century outbuilding.',
  'A six-bay house of coursed rubblestone and uncoursed ashlar with a wooden porch under the line of the main roof and low dormers; letters set into the brick of the west gable spell "E., I & H, 1773". A Greek Revival outbuilding stands behind, and a 1932 state marker at Hathorn Road and County Route 1A. The interior keeps Victorian furnishings (Wikipedia); it is not open.',
  'A Revolutionary militia general\'s 1773 stone house, his and his wife\'s initials in the gable.',
  'Private, on a 14-acre lot at 21 Hathorn Road, off NY 94 a mile south of Warwick village. View from Hathorn Road only.',
  [W('General John Hathorn Stone House'),HM('Hathorn House',149364)],
  brick='?'),
N('shingle-house-warwick','Shingle House','Warwick','midh','landmark','standing',41.25936,-74.35600,'exact','open','walk','','1764',
  'Daniel Burt built this saltbox in 1764; it is the oldest house in the village and a rare clapboard-and-shingle house for the region, after the Burts\' New England origins. Legend has it all the shingles came from one tree. It was the first property the Historical Society of the Town of Warwick acquired, in 1915 or 1916 (the society\'s page and Wikipedia differ).',
  'Much of the interior is original (Wikipedia). The complex at 7 Forester Avenue also holds the Sly Barn of about 1825 with a farm exhibit, a 1909 Lehigh & Hudson River "bobber" caboose, the 1906 Union African Methodist Episcopal Church moved here in 2007 with its original doors and windows, the Azariah Ketchum House and the Hasbrouck Carriage House.',
  'Warwick\'s oldest house, a 1764 saltbox shingled, says the legend, from a single tree.',
  'Historical Society of the Town of Warwick, 7 Forester Avenue in the village. Seen by private guided walking tour ($10 a person, five people or $50 minimum, booked ahead, no same- or next-day bookings) and at the society\'s open houses. The society\'s office is the 1810 House, 80 Main Street (its home page, read November 21, 2026). Street parking in the village.',
  [WVHD,WHS,L('Historical Society of the Town of Warwick: private walking tours','https://www.whsny.org/private-tours'),WHSH,L('Warwick Advertiser: Tour the oldest house in the Village of Warwick (2019)','https://warwickadvertiser.com/entertainment/tour-the-oldest-house-in-the-village-of-warwick-on-sept-29-AC532406')],
  conf='mixed',brick='some',hours='By private tour booked ahead; society office (1810 House) Tuesday, Wednesday, Friday 9 a.m. to 2 p.m.'),
N('bairds-tavern-warwick','Baird\'s Tavern','Warwick','midh','hotel','standing',41.25952,-74.35770,'exact','open','walk','','1766',
  'A stone tavern with a gambrel roof built in 1766 at the center of the village, on the King\'s Highway. George Washington reportedly stopped in for a drink of grog and John Trumbull passed through. It has been a house, a general store, offices and a gift shop; the Historical Society of the Town of Warwick owns it.',
  'Since 2017 the main floor is a working tavern again; the second floor and attic are restored historic rooms kept by the society, including a bedroom where Martha Washington is said to have stayed. The wooden floor keeps boot marks of the militia mustered here in the Revolution (Wikipedia).',
  'A 1766 stone tavern on the King\'s Highway that pours drinks again on its ground floor.',
  'Historical Society of the Town of Warwick, 103-105 Main Street. The tavern\'s opening hours were not checked (no page of its own was found); the upper rooms are seen by the society\'s private tours. Street parking in the village.',
  [WVHD,WHS,L('Historical Society of the Town of Warwick: private walking tours','https://www.whsny.org/private-tours')],
  brick='no',hours='?'),
N('old-school-baptist-meeting-house-warwick','Old School Baptist Meeting House','Warwick','midh','worship','standing',41.25812,-74.35834,'site','view','walk','','1810',
  'Eighteen Baptists from Connecticut came to Warwick in 1765 meaning to move on to northeastern Pennsylvania, but the Revolution kept them here; the congregation was organized in 1764 and its elder, James Benedict, ordained in 1765 (markers). The meeting house was built in 1810, partly by the master carpenter Azariah Ketchum. The Historical Society bought it for one dollar in 1951 to save it from demolition, and it appeared in the 1997 film In & Out.',
  'The society calls it the most photographed building in the town of Warwick. It stands at Main and High streets, with a 2016 Pomeroy marker; the society\'s older marker is on High Street. Interior not checked.',
  'The 1810 meeting house the historical society saved for a dollar, Warwick\'s most photographed building.',
  'Historical Society of the Town of Warwick. Rented for weddings and performances; no regular opening found. View from Main Street (NY 94) and High Street. The congregation\'s first burying ground, the 1795 Cemetery, is beside St. Stephen\'s Cemetery on NY 94.',
  [WVHD,WHS,HM('Meeting House',149365),HM('Old School Baptist Church',40607)],
  brick='?'),
N('sugar-loaf-historic-district','Sugar Loaf hamlet (Sugar Loaf Historic District)','Sugar Loaf, Town of Chester','midh','town','standing',41.31962,-74.28674,'area','public','walk','','about 1749',
  'Founded in the late 1740s (the community marker says about 1749) as a stopping place on the King\'s Highway in the Wawayanda Patent, selling food, goods and horses to travelers; the hamlet is named for the bald Sugar Loaf mountain above it. Hambletonian 10, sire of the American standardbred, was foaled at Sugar Loaf in 1848. Its 19th-century buildings and its revival as a craft village late in the 20th century made it a National Register district in November 2024.',
  'Kings Highway lined with 19th-century houses and shops, many of them artists\' and craft shops. Markers for the Register district, the community of craftsmen and the 1700s Sugar Loaf school district stand along Kings Highway near Pine Hill Road; Fury Brook Farm, the first farm on the old Wawayanda Path (1731), is about half a mile away.',
  'A King\'s Highway stopover of the 1740s that became a village of craftsmen.',
  'Public streets of the hamlet, in the Town of Chester. The pin is the district marker at 1388 Kings Highway. Shops keep their own hours. Parking along Kings Highway not checked.',
  [W('Sugar Loaf, New York'),HM('Sugarloaf Historic District',313488),HM('Sugar Loaf Community of Craftsmen',22431),L('The Chronicle: Sugar Loaf Historic District added to National Register (December 2024)','https://chroniclenewspaper.com/news/local-news/sugar-loaf-historic-district-added-to-national-register-of-historic-places-IF3979129')],
  brick='?'),
N('yelverton-inn-chester','Yelverton Inn and Store (Durland\'s)','Chester','midh','hotel','standing',41.35611,-74.27778,'site','view','walk','','1765',
  'A frame inn of 1765 that put up Alexander Hamilton and Aaron Burr during a local land dispute, and George Washington in the Revolution; Abijah Yelverton, its owner, gave the land for Chester\'s first Presbyterian church in 1783. Beside it is the 1841 store known locally as Durland\'s, a rare surviving Greek Revival commercial building. Inn, barn, shed and store were listed together on the National Register in 1980.',
  'The two-story L-shaped inn facing Main Street next to the First Presbyterian Church, with two Federal doorways; inside, original floors, doors and most trim, including unusual "tau-cross" door frames. Behind it a shed with seventeen round holes drilled below its gable cornice, and a three-story barn of brick and stone below and shingle above (Wikipedia). Interior not open; present use not checked.',
  'A 1765 inn where Hamilton, Burr and Washington stayed, with its 1841 Greek Revival store.',
  'On NY 94 where it turns from Main Street to Academy Street in Chester village. Ownership and use not checked: view from the street.',
  [W('Yelverton Inn and Store')],
  brick='?'),
N('first-presbyterian-chester','First Presbyterian Church of Chester','Chester','midh','worship','standing',41.35705,-74.27774,'exact','open','walk','','1854',
  'The congregation dates from 1783, when Abijah Yelverton of the inn next door gave an acre on a knoll southeast of here for a church. This Greek Revival church of 1854 is its third building, on its third site. A small frame chapel was added at the rear in 1884 and a stone education center in 1967. It was listed on the National Register in 1998.',
  'A white timber-frame church with a pedimented portico of Ionic columns and paneled corner pilasters toward NY 94, and an octagonal belfry. Stained glass went in in 1898 and an Estey pipe organ in 1925; otherwise the interior is unaltered from 1854 (Wikipedia). A marker stands in front.',
  'An 1854 Greek Revival church with its 1898 glass and 1925 Estey organ, little changed inside.',
  'Active Presbyterian church, 94-96 Main Street (NY 94), Chester. Its directions page gives Sunday worship at 10 a.m. in one line and 11 a.m. in another (read November 21, 2026): call ahead.',
  [W('First Presbyterian Church of Chester'),L('First Presbyterian Church of Chester: information and directions','https://chespresny.com/informationdirections/'),HM('First Presbyterian Church',22424)],
  brick='no',hours='Sunday worship 10 a.m. (the church\'s page also says 11 a.m.)'),
N('seward-memorial-florida','William Henry Seward Memorial','Florida','midh','landmark','standing',41.33367,-74.35712,'exact','public','walk','','1930',
  'A memorial to William Henry Seward, born in Florida, who as Lincoln\'s Secretary of State negotiated the purchase of Alaska. Daniel Chester French, sculptor of the seated Lincoln in Washington, made the bronze bust, set in a small circular plaza with benches designed by Richard Henry Dana III. It was unveiled on September 24, 1930, listed on the National Register in 1999 and restored in 2000; vandals shifted the bust and cracked a bench in 2007.',
  'The bronze bust in its round plaza on Main Street, with S. S. Seward Institute, the school named for his father, just behind. HMdb lists two Seward markers here, and the 1741 Presbyterian marker is a short walk north at Glenmere Avenue.',
  'Daniel Chester French\'s bust of the man who bought Alaska, in the village where he was born.',
  'Public plaza on Main Street (NY 17A/94) in the village of Florida. Street parking.',
  [W('William Henry Seward Memorial'),HM('William H. Seward',21082)],
  brick='?'),
]
